[{"id":"4532","date":"2026-11-13T07:10:46+00:00","text":"🧠 Some kids' math struggles may be about strategy, not numbers A Stanford University study published in JNeurosci suggests that children with developmental dyscalculia struggle not only with calculations but also with choosing how to approach a problem. Led by Oliver Lasnick, the team found that these children took longer to switch methods, were less responsive when difficulty changed, and — unlike other children — did not get better at choosing approaches over time. Brain activity in a particular system also differed between them and children more skilled at using strategies. The study followed 68 children between the ages of 8 and 10. The findings show differences in behavior and brain activity, not what causes them. #neuroscience #dyscalculia #childdevelopment @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/mG-BwrSEPBhO0QPgmtXAysHiPBMDe66AOysrV8lYCWWfbhpbxTqbHQ48WrM4JAhDcfI4P6ltpm27TpcFAXY-gY6TdlrZgdhvYtL_-XZtfe8cA5lxSbsyBE0RgLWlAlHliOjZuCQpEe7-2afYiAq8r3ZV54SrKEZJU5_VnVQ2Vl3s3A7_yXp9qZ1nmLp2ob1ilbCFWDDBbVU0qBdEdkNwhKTPUPDy3iZHfST0DorWL3v3wC2ucM2LQCjpW0szCGt_2wAskER0oXhcyUrUc-WtZBL9EFtT1UYAVYwCZw0Jyl_w8zs4W4sGC7acZDTxCh33xw5YJv5cVrUYz5xuyNMF6w.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4530","date":"2026-11-12T13:11:31+00:00","text":"","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/MoQ_aIwZD3e_peYHs7gwZiNH0CVThf4Cgj1LU44yLfU_h-qyl7zkoPPzGN0NtODbJaJfOGp5MVvY6txLC2iNA5G_WNIuTdeQohpTJSTrCUB9mH7QG2C2fkTKilfjg9sYPcqkxuJtPoqfk2bXVm-km-dMQ4XdJ9r0wuvRzZ7N-GMnDkwarxQ8CC_67bn89E8In0UdWRFFootLu-2u0vuDc_2aEpt9N87ltwaoyLWzKQFp6dSWdEY15CbyPjVKJ96PgrMNzJdj9ca7Ty1x0wzGZ4MAwdzltxrLMn1KuuoY0F1VPuY1g8VMLz9Gr3hhDPGLHSD7g4C2lUifOq5njI2QEg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4529","date":"2026-11-12T13:08:42+00:00","text":"","image":"","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4528","date":"2026-11-12T13:08:41+00:00","text":"","image":"","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4527","date":"2026-11-12T13:07:46+00:00","text":"","image":"","video":""