[{"id":"4534","date":"2026-11-13T15:50:59+00:00","text":"🦠 Salmonella programmed to attack tumours, with a gel to hold it back Researchers at Nanjing University engineered an attenuated Salmonella strain to carry anti-cancer nanobodies and paired it with a gel that keeps it under control, The Innovation reports. Tests were in mice. Tumours appeal to the bacteria — low oxygen, no gut competitors — but multiplying Salmonella poisons the liver and spleen. The gelator comes from isosteviol, a compound derived from stevia. It stays liquid in the syringe and sets in under a minute on meeting sodium in the abdominal cavity. Release is mechanical, not timed: at rest almost nothing, under gentle agitation about 80%. Bacteria-induced weight loss fell by 82% (78.8% for the nanobody strain), livers and spleens stayed normal, and in a two-tumour model the gel helped four of five mice against one of five without it. Caveats: mice only, it did not help every animal, and human trials are far off. Photo: Rawpixelcom/Shutterstock/FOTODOM #cancer #bacteria #nanobody @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/eJdDhNqPEZFjqkYTd7A_qNzSij9iW-IC2sY3MRZOq_j6ireOXtxZCSZZMp1uLKyKeo6GaC_VAmjyecI-rjR9Jhghka-1m6E8XjfPDbJTPryPtlZxOgHySc3e57XTKzx-RNPeIc0rJMFWef0Jh3yAatOm0PIxICltPRrO2dsywMOBISpn0_5iLCj45qFfWqatqG4wRG36djVeR2F15je-NQTKq_Hvti7w8T-M-5f2GzRUBeDi0fLWaOVZ4_zAQco5cQ5zRNOE1nm2_N5HGo4CIV0vmjfCCgevOmvL0fOb1PDxej0K1Gzg9RT-Mpc2ooQYRmoebojy5HzdlsxKQzmvyw.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4533","date":"2026-11-13T11:30:30+00:00","text":"🪓 A Siberian dig may hold traces of an unknown Bronze Age culture At Karyer Tai-1 near Novosibirsk, an expedition led by academician Vyacheslav Molodin excavated a Bronze Age sanctuary, with campfire traces and casting-mould fragments in a ditch, and sacrificed sable and fox bones in the pits. A burial pit held a vessel matching the Petrovka culture, which lay hundreds of kilometres to the south-west, and a mould for casting a Seima-Turbino-type bronze celt. Palaeogenetics at the Institute of Cytology and Genetics linked the local Baraba population to newcomers from modern Kazakhstan. Molodin says this may be a new culture of the developed Bronze Age — still a hypothesis, with digging resuming in 2027. #archaeology #bronzeage #siberia @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/ouNj70r5DhmI4bsKrT_QObxgT8Djl02AHKTzWg9Ow0LbKb07jZUYlc8pA-NZa8QppS4yOogGBs91I6CjX8OJzo2aol1UPAs9-bKGzWXaTY9cwkj3fwGWlHkvwj40B5D4G1oFH1dxJSrwPYEDZDfd2tAPCupFHVU5G4s8gY39_xGt3FJZ4yjgFizxYQuhXE1JxYDSCU-fNnZ7WIlWXh1ocrlX-7vSxRH7XkLnXZ_SL7RDVymN6SLr7Y4lgNZ6FG88wwMj8JR7snrzcz3f5zlJB_B4vF9W8EPa5cA-T4mGzFq8zkFQ7mvoNbWvfA3dwvxO2Cxf7b6mSeOx1OVmVvU_Nw.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4532","date":"2026-11-13T07:10:46+00:00","text":"🧠 Some kids' math struggles may be about strategy, not numbers A Stanford University study published in JNeurosci suggests that children with developmental dyscalculia struggle not only with calculations but also with choosing how to approach a problem. Led by Oliver Lasnick, the team found that these children took longer to switch methods, were less responsive when difficulty changed, and — unlike other children — did not get better at choosing approaches over time. Brain activity in a particular system also differed between them and children more skilled at using strategies. The study followed 68 children between the ages of 8 and 10. The findings show differences in behavior and brain activity, not what causes them. #neuroscience #dyscalculia #childdevelopment @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/mG-BwrSEPBhO0QPgmtXAysHiPBMDe66AOysrV8lYCWWfbhpbxTqbHQ48WrM4JAhDcfI4P6ltpm27TpcFAXY-gY6TdlrZgdhvYtL_-XZtfe8cA5lxSbsyBE0RgLWlAlHliOjZuCQpEe7-2afYiAq8r3ZV54SrKEZJU5_VnVQ2Vl3s3A7_yXp9qZ1nmLp2ob1ilbCFWDDBbVU0qBdEdkNwhKTPUPDy3iZHfST0DorWL3v3wC2ucM2LQCjpW0szCGt_2wAskER0oXhcyUrUc-WtZBL9EFtT1UYAVYwCZw0Jyl_w8zs4W4sGC7acZDTxCh33xw5YJv5cVrUYz5xuyNMF6w.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4530","date":"2026-11-1